## [ISSUE] Light-theme username colour in `.topic-meta-data h5 a` changed from 30% to 70%

**Severity:** MEDIUM — visible regression on the default light theme: bold username link becomes pale grey

### The Problem
```scss
// before
      color: scale-color($primary, $lightness: 30%);
// after (line 291)
      color: dark-light-choose(scale-color($primary, $lightness: 70%), scale-color($secondary, $lightness: 30%));
```
The first (light-theme) argument is `70%` instead of the original `30%`. The dark-theme argument `30%` looks like the percentages were swapped: under the PR's `100% - x` rule the pair should be `30%` / `70%`.

### Theory / Proof
`dark-light-choose` (`app/assets/stylesheets/common/foundation/variables.scss:46-52`) returns its **first** argument whenever `brightness($primary) < brightness($secondary)`, i.e. for every light theme, including the default one:

```scss
@function dark-light-choose($light-theme-result, $dark-theme-result) {
  @if brightness($primary) < brightness($secondary) {
    @return $light-theme-result;
  } @else {
    @return $dark-theme-result;
  }
}
```

So the first argument must be the exact expression that was there before, or the compiled CSS for light themes changes. The PR title says the goal is to fix dark themes; this line changes the light-theme output as well.
With the default `$primary: #222`, `scale-color(#222, $lightness: 30%)` is about `#646464`, while `70%` is about `#bcbcbc`. That is a large jump in lightness, and `#bcbcbc` on white is roughly 1.9:1 contrast, well below WCAG 2.x AA (4.5:1) for normal text.

### What happens today vs. what will break
Today (before the PR), light themes render the username link at about `#646464`. After the PR, every light-theme site renders it at about `#bcbcbc`, so the post header link becomes hard to read. Dark themes get `scale-color($secondary, 30%)`, which is dimmer than the `70%` mirror used everywhere else in this PR.

### Research
WCAG 2.1 SC 1.4.3 (Contrast Minimum) needs 4.5:1 for normal text. Almost every other converted line in this PR keeps the original `$primary` percentage as the first argument and uses `100% - x` on `$secondary` as the second (e.g. `60%` -> `dark-light-choose(... 60%, ... 40%)`). A scan of the whole diff found only five lines that break that rule: `desktop/topic-post.scss:291`, `mobile/modal.scss:102`, `mobile/topic-post.scss:182`, `desktop/user.scss:522` and `mobile/user.scss:497`.

### Recommendation
```scss
      color: dark-light-choose(scale-color($primary, $lightness: 30%), scale-color($secondary, $lightness: 70%));
```

## [ISSUE] Light-theme colour of `.custom-message-length` inverted (70% to 30%)

**Severity:** MEDIUM — mobile flag-modal character-count hint changes from light grey to near body-text darkness on light themes

### The Problem
```scss
// before
  color: scale-color($primary, $lightness: 70%);
// after (line 102)
  color: dark-light-choose(scale-color($primary, $lightness: 30%), scale-color($secondary, $lightness: 70%));
```
The two percentages are swapped. The light-theme branch should keep `70%`, and the dark-theme mirror should be `30%`.

### Theory / Proof
`dark-light-choose` (`app/assets/stylesheets/common/foundation/variables.scss:46-52`) returns its **first** argument whenever `brightness($primary) < brightness($secondary)`, i.e. for every light theme, including the default one:

```scss
@function dark-light-choose($light-theme-result, $dark-theme-result) {
  @if brightness($primary) < brightness($secondary) {
    @return $light-theme-result;
  } @else {
    @return $dark-theme-result;
  }
}
```

So the first argument must be the exact expression that was there before, or the compiled CSS for light themes changes. The PR title says the goal is to fix dark themes; this line changes the light-theme output as well.
With `$primary: #222`, `70%` gives about `#bcbcbc` (a faint secondary hint) and `30%` gives about `#646464` (close to normal secondary text). The helper text was meant to be de-emphasised. For comparison, the desktop counterpart `desktop/modal.scss` still uses the original light value in its version of this rule.

### What happens today vs. what will break
Before the PR, the mobile flag dialog's "N characters remaining" hint was faint grey. After it, light-theme users see it as dark grey, so it looks as important as the flag message itself. On dark themes it renders at `scale-color($secondary, 70%)`, which is brighter than intended (the mirror of 70% is 30%).

### Research
Look at the same rule in `app/assets/stylesheets/desktop/modal.scss` for the intended light value. Almost every other converted line in this PR keeps the original `$primary` percentage as the first argument and uses `100% - x` on `$secondary` as the second (e.g. `60%` -> `dark-light-choose(... 60%, ... 40%)`). A scan of the whole diff found only five lines that break that rule: `desktop/topic-post.scss:291`, `mobile/modal.scss:102`, `mobile/topic-post.scss:182`, `desktop/user.scss:522` and `mobile/user.scss:497`.

### Recommendation
```scss
  color: dark-light-choose(scale-color($primary, $lightness: 70%), scale-color($secondary, $lightness: 30%));
```

## [ISSUE] Mobile topic-map `h3` light-theme colour changed from 20% to 50%

**Severity:** LOW — h3 and h4 in the mobile topic map now render the same grey on light themes

### The Problem
```scss
// before
  h3 { ... color: scale-color($primary, $lightness: 20%); }
  h4 { ... color: scale-color($primary, $lightness: 50%); }
// after (line 182)
  h3 { ... color: dark-light-choose(scale-color($primary, $lightness: 50%), scale-color($secondary, $lightness: 50%)); }
```
The light-theme argument should be `20%`, and its dark mirror should be `80%`.

### Theory / Proof
`dark-light-choose` (`app/assets/stylesheets/common/foundation/variables.scss:46-52`) returns its **first** argument whenever `brightness($primary) < brightness($secondary)`, i.e. for every light theme, including the default one:

```scss
