  @if brightness($primary) < brightness($secondary) {
    @return $light-theme-result;
  } @else {
    @return $dark-theme-result;
  }
}
```

So the first argument must be the exact expression that was there before, or the compiled CSS for light themes changes. The PR title says the goal is to fix dark themes; this line changes the light-theme output as well.
Before the PR, `h3` (for example the counts in the topic map) was darker (`20%` ≈ `#4e4e4e`) than the `h4` labels (`50%` ≈ `#919191`). Now both come out at `#919191`, so the visual hierarchy is gone. Other converted lines in this same file keep their original percentage, for example `mobile/topic-post.scss:247` (`20%` / `80%`) and `:270` (`75%` / `25%`).

### What happens today vs. what will break
Light-theme mobile users see the topic-map numbers fade to the same grey as their labels. On dark themes the h3 gets `secondary 50%` instead of the `80%` mirror.

### Research
Other `20%` lines in this PR, for example `mobile/topic-post.scss:247`, are converted to the correct `20%` / `80%` pair. Almost every other converted line in this PR keeps the original `$primary` percentage as the first argument and uses `100% - x` on `$secondary` as the second (e.g. `60%` -> `dark-light-choose(... 60%, ... 40%)`). A scan of the whole diff found only five lines that break that rule: `desktop/topic-post.scss:291`, `mobile/modal.scss:102`, `mobile/topic-post.scss:182`, `desktop/user.scss:522` and `mobile/user.scss:497`.

### Recommendation
```scss
    color: dark-light-choose(scale-color($primary, $lightness: 20%), scale-color($secondary, $lightness: 80%));
```

## [ISSUE] Group member `.name` light-theme colour changed from 30% to 50% (desktop and mobile)

**Severity:** LOW — member name and title now render the same grey on light themes

### The Problem
```scss
// before
      .name  { ... color: scale-color($primary, $lightness: 30%); }
      .title { ... color: scale-color($primary, $lightness: 50%); }
// after (desktop/user.scss:522, same change in mobile/user.scss:497)
      .name  { ... color: dark-light-choose(scale-color($primary, $lightness: 50%), scale-color($secondary, $lightness: 50%)); }
```
The first argument should stay at `30%`, with a `70%` dark mirror.

### Theory / Proof
`dark-light-choose` (`app/assets/stylesheets/common/foundation/variables.scss:46-52`) returns its **first** argument whenever `brightness($primary) < brightness($secondary)`, i.e. for every light theme, including the default one:

```scss
@function dark-light-choose($light-theme-result, $dark-theme-result) {
  @if brightness($primary) < brightness($secondary) {
    @return $light-theme-result;
  } @else {
    @return $dark-theme-result;
  }
}
```

So the first argument must be the exact expression that was there before, or the compiled CSS for light themes changes. The PR title says the goal is to fix dark themes; this line changes the light-theme output as well.
The `.name` / `.title` pair was deliberately styled at two levels (30% vs 50%) so the member's name stands out from their title. Changing `.name` to 50% makes both the same colour. The identical change in `mobile/user.scss:497` shows the edit was copy-pasted rather than being a deliberate redesign.

### What happens today vs. what will break
The group members list on light themes loses the difference between name and title. On dark themes the name gets `secondary 50%` instead of the `70%` mirror.

### Research
Every other converted line in these two files keeps its original percentage. Almost every other converted line in this PR keeps the original `$primary` percentage as the first argument and uses `100% - x` on `$secondary` as the second (e.g. `60%` -> `dark-light-choose(... 60%, ... 40%)`). A scan of the whole diff found only five lines that break that rule: `desktop/topic-post.scss:291`, `mobile/modal.scss:102`, `mobile/topic-post.scss:182`, `desktop/user.scss:522` and `mobile/user.scss:497`.

### Recommendation
```scss
        color: dark-light-choose(scale-color($primary, $lightness: 30%), scale-color($secondary, $lightness: 70%));
// apply the same fix at app/assets/stylesheets/mobile/user.scss:497
```
